      "type": "text",
      "text": "1 Introduction\r\n1.1 Applicability\r\n1.2 Background\r\n1.3 Language and Compliance Requirements\r\n2 Environmental and Social Safeguards\r\n2.1 Safeguarding of Storage Sites\r\n2.2 Co-Benefits\r\n3 Biochar Characterization\r\n3.1 Overview\r\n3.2 Physical Characteristics\r\n3.3 Chemical Characteristics\r\n4 Sampling Guidance, Laboratory Requirements, Data Quality\r\n4.1 Sampling Guidance\r\n4.1.1 Homogeneity Considerations\r\n4.1.2 Laboratory Requirements\r\n4.2 Analytical Checks, Calibration, and QA/QC\r\n4.3 Data Reporting\r\n5 Durability of Biochar in Soils\r\n5.1 Quantification of Biochar Durability\r\n5.1.1 Quantification of CO2estored\r\n5.1.2 Calculation of Corg\r\n5.1.2.1 Measurement of Mass of Biochar Applied\r\n5.1.2.2 Calculation of Fdurable\r\n5.1.2.2.1 Option 1: 200 Year Durability\r\n5.1.2.2.2 Option 2: 1,000 Year Durability\r\n5.1.3 Combining Durability Options\r\n5.2 Environmental Monitoring\r\n5.2.1 Site Management\r\n5.2.2 Baseline Establishment\r\n5.2.2.1 Site Selection to Minimizing Reversal Risks\r\n6 Proof of Biochar End Use\r\n6.1 Calculation of CO2eEmissions,RP\r\n7 Buffer Pool and Reversal Risk\r\n7.1 Buffer Pool\r\n7.2 Risk of Post-Deployment Use as Fuel\r\n7.3 Biochar Stockpiling Duration and Safety\r\n8 Appendix I: Overview of Required Measurement to Credit Using This Module\r\n9 Appendix II: Guidance on Evidence for Biochar Application or Mixing\r\n9.1 Introduction\r\n9.2 Crediting at the Point of Mixing and Transfer\r\n9.2.1 Principle\r\n9.2.2 Requirements for Crediting at Point of Mixing\r\n9.3 Definitions\r\n9.4 General Principles for Evidence\r\n9.5 Acceptable Evidence for Biochar Application Directly to Soil\r\n9.5.1 Visual Documentation\r\nContents\r\n03/02/2026, 10:56 Biochar Storage in Soil Environments v1.2 — Isometric\r\nhttps://registry.isometric.com/module/biochar-storage-soil-environments/1.2 2/72"
