      "text": "5.0\r\nDurability of Biochar in Soils\r\n5.1\r\nQuantification of Biochar Durability\r\nThere are two options for calculating the fraction of durable biochar\r\n( ) in this Module. Option 1 results in Credits issued at 200\r\nyear durability, and Option 2 results in Credits issued at 1,000 year\r\ndurability.\r\nIn either case, the formula to calculate CO e is:\r\n(Equation 1)\r\nWhere\r\nis the carbon content of the biochar (empirical).\r\nis the dry mass of biochar applied.\r\nis the fraction of durable biochar that remains in the soil\r\nfor the full duration of the crediting timeline (i.e. either 200 or\r\n1,000 years), and can be credited under this Module.\r\nis the mass fraction of carbon dioxide and elemental\r\ncarbon.\r\n5.1.1\r\nQuantification of CO2estored\r\nFdurable\r\n2 stored\r\nCO2estored = Cbiochar × mbiochar × Fdurable ×\r\n12.01\r\n44.01\r\nCbiochar\r\nmbiochar\r\nFdurable\r\n12.01\r\n44.01\r\nThis Module only Credits for the durably stored fraction in\r\nbiochar, which is used to calculate . While biochar\r\nassociated inorganic carbon generally makes up a small fraction of\r\ntotal biochar C. However, the fate of biochar-associated is\r\nmuch less predictable in soils . Thus, is calculated using\r\nthe following equation:\r\n(Equation 2)\r\nWhere:\r\n5.1.2\r\nCalculation of Corg\r\nCorg\r\nCO2estored\r\nCinorg\r\n29,30 Cbiochar\r\nCbiochar = Total Carbon Content − Cinorg\r\n03/02/2026, 10:56 Biochar Storage in Soil Environments v1.2 — Isometric\r\nhttps://registry.isometric.com/module/biochar-storage-soil-environments/1.2 24/72"
    },
