      "type": "text",
      "text": "geologically stable carbon. Using petrographic analysis, Sanei et al.,\r\n(2024) identified that biochars with a mean random reflectance (R\r\n≥ 2%) exhibit structural characteristics equivalent to inertinite\r\nmacerals in fossil coals and chars, which are known to persist over\r\ngeological timescales. While biochar meeting the benchmark of R ≥\r\n2% can be considered permanent, additional peer-reviewed\r\nresearch has been published that further validates the\r\nexperimental work of Sanei et al., (2024) .\r\nAs outlined in Section 3 of this Module, Project Proponents must\r\nreport a set of at least 500 measurements of R , calculated at the\r\nmaceral-level, for at least three replicate samples of their biochar.\r\nBatches that adopt this measurement approach can be credited for\r\nthe percentage of their biochar which passes the 2% R benchmark,\r\nas outlined in Sanei et al. (2024) . The histogram of the values must\r\nbe submitted at the point of project verification for this Crediting\r\noption. This method was further updated in Sanei et al., (2025) to\r\nrefine the methodology to only account for the recalcitrant fraction of\r\nbiochar (discounting the reactive fraction, determined by\r\nthermogravimetric analysis).\r\nTo ensure a conservative approach when Crediting biochar\r\ndurability, we account for uncertainty in both R measurement, and\r\nthe proportion of carbon that is non-reactive. Specifically, the\r\ncredited durable fraction ( ) is calculated using the mean\r\nvalues for each parameter reduced by one standard deviation. This\r\nmethod ensures that the durability estimate reflects a lower-bound\r\nconfidence level, mitigating the risk of overestimating long-term\r\ncarbon storage.\r\nAs such, is calculated using through:\r\nCalculating the sample standard deviation of quantifying the\r\ntypical deviation of individual measurements from their mean,\r\nwhich is used to account for uncertainty and conservatively adjust\r\nthe estimated durable fraction of biochar carbon, as:\r\n(Equation 4)\r\nWhere:\r\nis the standard deviation of measurements\r\nis the number of measurements in the sample (i.e. >3).\r\nis the individual measurement of for the -th sample.\r\nis the mean of all measurements.\r\nCalculating the sample standard deviation of\r\nquantifying the typical deviation of individual\r\nmeasurements from their mean, which is used to account for\r\n11\r\n0\r\n0\r\n33,34,35\r\n11\r\n0\r\n0\r\n11\r\n0\r\n36\r\n0\r\nFdurable\r\nFdurable\r\nR0\r\nR0\r\nsR0\r\n= (R − ) nR − 1 0\r\n1\r\ni=1\r\n∑\r\nnR0\r\n0,i R0ˉ 2\r\nsR0 R0\r\nnR0\r\nR0,i i\r\nR0ˉ R0\r\nCnon−reactive\r\nCnon−reactive\r\n03/02/2026, 10:56 Biochar Storage in Soil Environments v1.2 — Isometric\r\nhttps://registry.isometric.com/module/biochar-storage-soil-environments/1.2 28/72"
