      "text": "Robert Brown, Ph.D.\r\nRyan Penney, Ph.D.\r\nSophie Gill, Ph.D.\r\nEmma Marsland\r\nJasmin Kazazi\r\nChris Pollard\r\nMarya Matlin-Wainer\r\nv1.2\r\n36. Sanei, H., Wojtaszek-Kalaitzidi, M., Schovsbo, N. H., Stenshøj, R.,\r\nZhou, Z., Schmidt, H.-P., Hagemann, N., Chiaramonti, D., Kiaitsis, T.,\r\nRudra, A., Lehner, A. J., Brown, R. W., Gill, S., Dorr, E., Kalaitzidis, S.,\r\nGoodarzi, F., & Petersen, H. I. (2025). Quantifying inertinite carbon\r\nin biochar. International Journal of Coal Geology, 310, 104886.\r\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.coal.2025.104886 ↩\r\n37. Dynarski, K. A., Bossio, D. A., & Scow, K. M. (2020). Dynamic\r\nstability of soil carbon: Reassessing the “permanence” of soil\r\ncarbon sequestration. Frontiers in Environmental Science, 8,\r\n514701. https://doi.org/10.3389/fenvs.2020.514701 ↩\r\n38. Weng, Z. H., & Cowie, A. L. (2025). Estimates vary but credible\r\nevidence points to gigaton-scale climate change mitigation\r\npotential of biochar. Communications Earth & Environment, 6(1),\r\n259. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02228-x ↩\r\n39. Adhikari, S., Moon, E., Paz-Ferreiro, J., & Timms, W. (2024).\r\nComparative analysis of biochar carbon stability methods and\r\nimplications for carbon credits. Science of The Total\r\nEnvironment, 914, 169607.\r\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.169607 ↩\r\n40. De Rosa, D., Ballabio, C., Lugato, E., Fasiolo, M., Jones, A., &\r\nPanagos, P. (2023). Soil organic carbon stocks in European\r\ncroplands and grasslands: How much have we lost in the past\r\ndecade? Global Change Biology, 30(2), e16992.\r\nhttps://doi.org/10.1111/gcb.16992 ↩\r\n41. Singh, H., Northup, B. K., Rice, C. W., et al. (2022). Biochar\r\napplications influence soil physical and chemical properties,\r\nmicrobial diversity, and crop productivity: A meta-analysis.\r\nBiochar, 4, 8. https://doi.org/10.1007/s42773-022-00138-1 ↩\r\nContributors\r\nChangelog\r\nCERTIFIED\r\n03/02/2026, 10:56 Biochar Storage in Soil Environme